00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:00.789 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the stock market is back up after  falling off a cliff  earlier this year you know we've had  these furious rallies investors continue  to put their money into our stock market  you're seeing new record highs on the s  p  and nasdaq major indexes haven't just  recovered from the coronavirus shock  they're on a historic winning streak but  while stocks climbed  millions lost their jobs and fell out of  the labor force  companies say more layoffs could come  this fall  this means that the wealthiest americans  are netting huge returns  while the poorest are losing  opportunities to make money i think the  question on  everyone's mind has been how is the  economy  so bad while the stock market is soaring  higher and higher  to get a sense for the disconnect you  can look at the labor market  we still have 11 million uh people  out of the 22 million who were laid off  from  in the payroll numbers in march and  april still 11 million out there  and that's that's really good progress  we've we've put fully half of those back  to work but that's a long way to go  our economy had come to a standstill  during the pandemic  americans are getting laid off they're  making less money they're spending  less money and all of that hurts our  economy about half of the workers in the  leisure and hospitality industry  lost their jobs this summer casinos  airports and music venues  sat virtually empty that's weighing on  consumer spending  it plummeted in march and still hasn't  reached pre-pandemic levels  experts say that this is unlikely to  change until the virus is contained  that means businesses are making fewer  sales and hiring fewer people  there were 10.7 million fewer us jobs in  september  versus february that will make it more  difficult for the unemployed to find  work  despite a crumbling economy the stock  market has soared  and there are a few forces driving that  rally  most notable were the policies coming  out of america's central bank  we acted forcefully to get our markets  working again and as a result  market conditions have generally  improved in march  the fed made emergency loans available  to banks local governments and other  players in the global economy  that kept investors optimistic about the  future and that  can drive up the markets investors said  the fed stepped in  like they'd never had before their  response was quicker  and more muscular than ever before and  that was a  key driver for the stock market this  spring  the bank started by slashing interest  rates to near zero which made it cheaper  to borrow money  then the bank started to buy debt by  september the fed controlled seven  trillion dollars worth of assets  up from four trillion at the beginning  of the pandemic  over coming months we will continue to  increase our holdings of treasury  securities and agency mortgage-backed  securities  at least at the current pace these asset  purchases are intended to sustain  smooth market functioning and help  foster accommodated financial conditions  the bank's purchasing has sent yields on  traditionally safe government debt  to near all-time lows that's driving  investors towards stocks  treasuries all of a sudden don't look as  attractive because the yield on them  is so low so if you want returns  you might as well go to stocks and  that's kind of the calculus that a lot  of investors have been doing  and it's actually people call it the  puerto quotina effect there is no  alternative to stocks because  yields are just so low where else are  you going to go for returns  this and other actions from the fed  propped up the markets and the money  that flowed from these policies went  into one section of the market in  particular  technology tech stocks lifted entire  market indexes this year  you can see that by comparing the tech  heavy nasdaq and s  p indices to the performance of the dow  jones industrial average  it's easy to think like wow stocks are  doing so well but  what we've seen this year is that it's  really a handful of stocks that are  doing really well and  those are mainly technology companies  apple microsoft amazon  facebook alphabet these big names offer  products that were crucial to live life  and conduct business in the pandemic  and their leaders took note on earnings  calls this summer this was a strong  quarter for us especially  compared to what we expected at the  start we began to see a significant  increase in customer demand beginning in  early march  in an uncertain environment apple saw a  quarter of historic results  investors expect solid earnings from  these companies which will boost demand  for their stock  you really can't overstate how well  these tech companies have done this year  and what a big role that plays in  driving the s p 500 up higher  apple alone recently was worth more than  all of the stocks in the ftse 100 index  or  all of the companies in this russell  2000 index of small stocks  tech stocks are doing so well their  gains are outweighing losses and  financial companies and energy stocks  which haven't been able to rally smaller  businesses are hurting too  bill ackman of pershing square holdings  said that if there were a stock market  index of private small businesses  it would likely be down 50 or more  while investors lock in expectations for  earnings in tech  a flood of action from individual  investors is also changing the markets  you're seeing newbie investors who had  never traded before they were stuck at  home during the pandemic and they  opened up brokerage accounts because  it's never been easier to trade it's  never been easier to open account  digital brokers like robinhood offer  commission free trading which has drawn  more investors into the market  during the first six months of the year  individual investors made up about 20  of market activity that's about a five  percentage point jump from last year  analysts said smaller investors drove up  stocks like nikola and eastman kodak  but they don't impact bigger names like  apple and tesla  brokers are making markets more  accessible but that's not helping the  bottom half of american workers  workers from lower income households  were more likely to lose their jobs in  the pandemic  and many in this group have historically  been shut out of the stock market  most stocks are held by the top 10 of  americans by wealth  according to data from the federal  reserve charts like this  sum up why a booming stock market seems  disconnected from the economic reality  for many americans

